---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const share = assertion('one-renter-household-in-six')
const ring = assertion('more-subsidized-housing-than-the-five-around-it')
const fewer = assertion('the-same-homes-hold-fewer-people')

const spec = (a: typeof share, yTitle: string) =>
  bar(
    a.figures.map((f) => f.label),
    a.figures.map((f) => f.value),
    { yTitle, labelValues: true },
  )

const shareSpec = spec(share, 'Per cent of renter households')
const ringSpec = spec(ring, 'Subsidized units per 1,000 residents')
const fewerSpec = spec(fewer, 'Per cent fall, 2009 to 2025')
---

<Article slug="one-renter-in-four">
  <p>
    Two thousand two hundred and fifty-one homes in Allen County have a federal subsidy attached to
    the rent. A thousand of those subsidies are vouchers a tenant carries to a private landlord;
    nine hundred and seventy are contracts on particular apartment buildings; two hundred and
    forty-three are houses a public authority owns. Together they hold four thousand two hundred
    and sixty-six people, which is one resident of this county in twenty-four.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={share} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="share"
    title="Subsidized homes as a share of renter households"
    caption="Subsidized units on 31 December 2025 against renter-occupied households in the 2023 five-year American Community Survey. The county's renter count carries a margin of ±619 and Lima's ±441, so these shares are good to about a point."
    columns={['Geography', 'Share of renter households']}
    rows={share.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="share" set:html={JSON.stringify(shareSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Lima holds 1,938 of the 2,251 — eighty-six per cent of the county's subsidized housing in a city
    that holds a third of its people. Bluffton has 43, Spencerville 38, Fort Shawnee 7, Elida 2. The
    remaining 223 are scattered across unincorporated township ground.
  </p>

  <h2>The county number is about the city</h2>
  <p>
    Allen County holds more subsidized housing than Hancock, Hardin, Auglaize, Van Wert and Putnam
    together — 2,251 units against 1,733 — on less than half their combined population. Read as a
    fact about the county, that is a claim about its character. It is really a claim about its
    geography: this is the county with the city in it, and those five are the ring of farms around
    it.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={ring} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="ring"
    title="Subsidized units per thousand residents"
    caption="Population is the Census Bureau's Vintage 2024 estimate. Cuyahoga County, the highest in Ohio, is 31.4 on the same measure; the lowest, Paulding, is 0.91."
    columns={['Geography', 'Units per 1,000']}
    rows={ring.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(1)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="ring" set:html={JSON.stringify(ringSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Ohio's eighty-eight counties run from 0.9 subsidized units per thousand residents to 31.4, a
    span of thirty. Allen County's own two halves run from 4.7 to 55.9, a span of fifty-one. The
    county is more various inside itself, on this measure, than the whole state is between its
    counties, and the single number that averages the two — 22.3, eighteenth in Ohio — is true of
    neither Lima nor the townships.
  </p>
  <Gloss>
    That is now a rule here rather than an observation. Before a county rate gets ranked against
    other counties, the same rate is computed for the largest subdivision and for the remainder; if
    the range inside is wider than the range between, the split is published and the rank is not.
    The housing pages had already said something like it in passing about owner-occupancy, and then
    the next measure got ranked against Ohio anyway.
  </Gloss>

  <h2>The same homes, five hundred fewer people</h2>
  <p>
    The stock has hardly moved since 2009. It was 2,401 units then and it is 2,251 now, and in
    seventeen years it has never left the band between 2,173 and 2,401. The number of people living
    in it has not been so steady: 4,801 then, 4,266 now.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={fewer} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="fewer"
    title="Subsidized units and their occupants, 2009 to 2025"
    caption="Per cent fall over seventeen years. Average household size in subsidized housing went from 2.2 people to 2.0 over the same span."
    columns={['Measure', 'Fall since 2009']}
    rows={fewer.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="fewer" set:html={JSON.stringify(fewerSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    The households changed rather than the buildings. In 2016 twenty-one per cent of them were
    headed by someone aged 62 or over; today thirty-two per cent are. In 2008 forty-seven per cent
    were a woman with children; today thirty-six per cent are. An elderly household is smaller than
    a family, and that is where the five hundred went.
  </p>
  <p>
    Their money moved in two directions at once. Average household income among the county's
    assisted households rose from $9,726 in 2009 to $14,640 in 2025 — half again in sixteen years.
    Over exactly the same span, the share of them earning less than thirty per cent of the area's
    median income rose from seventy-six per cent to eighty. Both sentences describe the same
    households. Their incomes went up in dollars and down against the county they live in.
  </p>
  <p>
    What none of this shows is the queue. The federal files publish an average waiting time, but it
    is the average wait of the households who <em>moved in</em>, so an authority that admits almost
    nobody reports a short one. How many families in Allen County are waiting for one of these 2,251
    doors is not a number any of these files contains.
  </p>
</Article>
